erefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(- \\frac{3}{2})=-1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(- \\frac{3}{2})=-1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{4x} \\\\ -1 & =A{e}^{4\\cdot (- \\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ -1 & =A{e}^{-6} \\\\ \\frac{-1}{{e}^{-6}} & =A \\\\ A & =\\frac{-1}{{e}^{-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535313 17592186045443 true] [17592186493344 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493341 13194139535313 17592186045443 true] [17592186493344 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535313 17592186045443 true] [17592186493344 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-y},\\,y(2)=-2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+x)$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" 13194139535313 17592186045443 true] [17592186493344 1072 #uuid "6119d730-e0d1-5eb1-8fa9-da95fad116e4" 13194139535313 17592186045443 true] [17592186493344 1074 "We're given that $y=\\ln(A+x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(2)=-2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(2)=-2$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\ln(A+x) \\\\ -2 & =\\ln(A+2) \\\\ {e}^{-2} & ={e}^{\\ln(A+2)} \\\\ e^{-2} & =A+2 \\\\ A & ={e}^{-2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535313 17592186045443 true] [17592186493345 1080 "\\frac{e^{2}}{3}" 13194139535313 17592186045443 true] [17592186493345 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535313 17592186045443 true] [17592186493345 1083 #uuid "61ba7b7d-5f48-5e29-965c-04ab9aa08574" 13194139535313 17592186045443 true] [17592186493346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493338 13194139535313 17592186045443 true] [17592186493346 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535313 17592186045443 true] [17592186493346 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}